//BUG="read_str loops forever when the input runs out"
//OUT="[hello]"
//IN="hello"

// Not in FEEDBACK.md. `read_str` (src/core/io.cpl) is
//
//     res = Str()
//     while c != ' ' && c != '\n'
//         res.push(c)
//         c = _builtin_getchar()
//
// and nothing in it looks for end of input. `_builtin_getchar` is C's
// `getchar`, so at EOF it yields -1, which is neither a space nor a newline —
// the loop never ends. It pushes -1 into the string forever, doubling the
// vector until the 1 GB bump arena is exhausted, so the program hangs and then
// dies rather than returning the word it already read.
//
// The input here is the word "hello" with no trailing newline, which is the
// smallest way to reach it, but the same loop hangs whenever a program asks
// for one more token than the input holds — a wrong loop bound turns into a
// silent hang instead of a wrong answer, which is the worst way for a
// competitive-programming submission to fail. There is also no way to write
// "read words until EOF", because nothing in the library reports it.
//
// `read_i64` is fine: its digit loop ends on any non-digit, so at EOF it
// returns 0. Only `read_str` spins.
//
// NOTE: until this is fixed the case costs a full run timeout (ten seconds by
// default, see CPLANG_TEST_TIMEOUT_SECS) because the harness has to wait the
// program out. That is the bug, not the harness.

fn main
    s = read_str()
    out "[{s}]"
